insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('a2-f-1100','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$A team modifies an open-source utility released under the GPL and uses the modified version only inside their own company, with no plan to give it to anyone outside the organization. Does the GPL require them to publish their source-code changes?$$,$$No - the GPL's source-disclosure obligation is triggered by distributing the software or a derivative work outside the organization. Purely internal modification and use, with nothing given to an outside party, does not trigger the requirement to release source code.$$),
('a2-f-1101','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$What is the practical difference between a permissive open-source license (like MIT or BSD) and a copyleft license (like the GPL)?$$,$$A permissive license lets anyone reuse, modify, and even relicense the code as proprietary/closed-source with minimal conditions (usually just keeping the copyright notice). A copyleft license requires that a distributed derivative work stay under the same open license, so proprietary software can't absorb the code and close it off.$$),
('a2-f-1102','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$A vendor's EULA forbids reverse engineering, decompiling, and redistributing its software. What kind of license is this, and how does it differ from most open-source licenses?$$,$$This is a commercial/proprietary license. Its EULA restricts what a user may do with the software - typically no reverse engineering, redistribution, or modification. Open-source licenses (permissive or copyleft) explicitly permit inspecting, modifying, and redistributing the source code, within whatever conditions that specific license sets.$$),
('a2-f-1103','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$A technician replaces a failed motherboard in a PC that shipped with an OEM copy of Windows. Why might the license fail to reactivate on the new board?$$,$$OEM licenses are typically tied to the first major hardware component they're activated on (commonly the motherboard) and generally aren't transferable to different hardware. A retail/full-use license, by contrast, can usually be moved to new hardware.$$),
('a2-f-1104','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$A user buys a DRM-protected e-book and wants to copy the file to a friend's e-reader from a different vendor. Why doesn't this simply work?$$,$$Digital Rights Management wraps the content with restrictions - often encryption tied to a specific account or authorized app - so it can't be freely copied and opened anywhere. Moving it usually requires the vendor's own transfer or authorization process, if the license even permits that at all.$$),
('a2-f-1105','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$How does a volume license differ from a standard single-user retail license?$$,$$A volume license lets an organization deploy one agreement's terms across many machines or seats under a single key or portal, often with centralized activation and license-count tracking. A retail license typically covers one installation tied to one user or device.$$),
('a2-f-1106','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$An employee asks IT to install a company-licensed application on their personally-owned laptop. What should be checked before doing anything technical?$$,$$Whether the software's license terms and the organization's personally-owned device policy actually permit that use - many licenses are scoped to company-owned assets only, and a personal-device policy may also require the user to accept monitoring or remote-wipe terms before IT installs or manages anything on it.$$),
('a2-f-1107','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$A technician discovers what appears to be illegal content on a customer's device during a repair. What must they do with the device itself?$$,$$Stop working on it immediately, avoid opening or accessing the content any further, and preserve it exactly as found - secure and label it so no one else touches it, document who found it and when, and report through the proper channel rather than deleting it or investigating further.$$),
('a2-f-1108','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$What is chain of custody, and why does a broken chain matter?$$,$$Chain of custody is the unbroken, documented record of who possessed a piece of evidence, when, and what was done with it, from discovery to final disposition. A broken chain (undocumented gaps or unauthorized access) puts the evidence's integrity in doubt, potentially making it inadmissible or untrustworthy in any legal or disciplinary proceeding.$$),
('a2-f-1109','aplus2','core',4,'4.5',$$What core details belong in incident response documentation after a security or policy-violation incident is handled?$$,$$What was reported and by whom, the date/time first noticed, the systems or data affected, who responded and what actions were taken in order, what evidence was collected and how it was preserved, and the final resolution or escalation outcome - detailed enough to support a later legal, HR, or audit review.$$),
('a2-f-1110','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.5',$$DRM$$,$$Digital Rights Management - technology (encryption, activation checks, licensing servers) that restricts copying, sharing, or transferring purchased digital content between devices or accounts.$$),
('a2-f-1111','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.5',$$GPL$$,$$GNU General Public License - a copyleft open-source license requiring that any distributed derivative work also be released under the GPL with its source code available, unlike permissive licenses such as MIT or BSD.$$),
('a2-f-1112','aplus2','feynman',4,'4.5',$$Explain why installing a single-user retail license on ten office computers creates real risk for a company, even if nobody ever gets caught.$$,$$Model answer: the EULA is a contract limiting use to the licensed count, so exceeding it is breach of contract and, in most jurisdictions, copyright infringement for the unauthorized copies. That risk exists independent of detection - a vendor audit, a disgruntled-employee tip, or routine license-reconciliation software can surface it years later, at which point the company owes back-fees, may face statutory damages, and risks reputational harm. Licensing compliance is a standing liability, not a one-time gamble, which is why organizations size volume licenses (with headroom) to their actual deployment counts instead.$$),
('a2-f-1113','aplus2','feynman',4,'4.5',$$Explain, using the discovered-illegal-content scenario, why a technician's instinct to 'just delete it and move on' is exactly the wrong response.$$,$$Model answer: deleting the content destroys evidence a legal or investigative process may need, and it puts the technician - not just the original device owner - into an undocumented chain of possession of that material. Continuing normal repair work risks further, unintentional access to it as well. The correct instinct reverses both errors: stop touching the device, preserve it exactly as found, document who found what and when, and hand it off through the defined reporting channel (a supervisor, legal, or law enforcement) so someone with actual authority - not the technician alone - decides what happens next.$$),
('a2-f-1114','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$What does 'active listening' mean in a support interaction, and what is a simple way to demonstrate it?$$,$$Fully focusing on what the customer is saying without interrupting or mentally drafting your response while they talk, then demonstrating you understood by restating the problem in your own words before proceeding - it confirms you captured the real issue rather than your assumption of it.$$),
('a2-f-1115','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$A customer looks confused after a technician says, 'Sounds like the spooler crashed and the print queue's holding a corrupt job.' What went wrong, and what should the technician do instead?$$,$$The explanation used technical jargon a non-technical customer likely won't follow, which can confuse them and leave them unable to act on any instructions given. Translating it into plain language - 'the printer's internal queue got stuck, so I'll clear it and try again' - keeps the customer informed and confident without talking down to them.$$),
('a2-f-1116','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$A repair requires ordering a backordered part. What is the professional way to set expectations with the customer about timing?$$,$$Give a realistic, specific estimate based on known facts, such as the part's actual lead time, noting it may shift - rather than a vague reassurance like 'it'll be done soon.' Honest expectations, even when the news isn't great, build trust; vague promises erode it the moment they aren't met.$$),
('a2-f-1117','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$A customer raises their voice and blames the technician personally for a problem that keeps recurring. What is the professional response?$$,$$Stay calm, avoid matching their tone or arguing back, listen fully to their frustration, acknowledge it directly - 'I understand this is frustrating, especially since it's happened before' - and then redirect toward a concrete next step. Reacting defensively or dismissively escalates the situation instead of resolving it.$$),
('a2-f-1118','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$A technician realizes they will be fifteen minutes late to a scheduled onsite appointment. What should they do?$$,$$Proactively call or message the customer as soon as the delay is known, give an honest reason and an updated arrival time, and apologize for the inconvenience - notifying them respects their time and prevents them from wondering, or complaining, about an apparent no-show.$$),
('a2-f-1119','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$Why should a technician silence personal calls and texts while actively working with a customer, rather than answering them quickly?$$,$$Distractions signal disrespect for the customer's time and problem, break concentration on the actual issue (risking mistakes), and undermine the customer's confidence that they have the technician's full attention - professionalism requires staying present and focused during the interaction.$$),
('a2-f-1120','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$A customer insists their problem is caused by something the technician's diagnostics clearly show isn't true. What is the professional way to handle the disagreement?$$,$$Acknowledge the customer's concern respectfully, calmly walk them through the evidence without being dismissive or condescending, and offer to keep investigating if they remain unconvinced - arguing to 'win,' or bluntly telling the customer they're wrong, damages the relationship even when the technician is technically correct.$$),
('a2-f-1121','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$Why does professional support communication call for neutral, culturally sensitive language regardless of who the customer is?$$,$$Customers come from many backgrounds, and careless jokes, assumptions, or comments tied to region, gender, or age can alienate or offend someone regardless of intent, damaging trust and the organization's reputation - sticking to respectful, neutral, work-focused language keeps every interaction professional.$$),
('a2-f-1122','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$While troubleshooting, a technician notices sensitive company financial data displayed on a customer's screen. What confidentiality rule applies?$$,$$Whatever a technician sees or learns while working on a customer's system is confidential by default - it should not be discussed with coworkers, friends, or on social media, and should only be shared through official channels if it is genuinely relevant to resolving or escalating the issue.$$),
('a2-f-1123','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$After remotely resolving a customer's issue, what professional step is often skipped but shouldn't be?$$,$$A brief proactive follow-up - confirming the fix is still holding and that the customer feels the issue is fully resolved - closes the loop, shows continued care beyond simply marking the ticket closed, and catches any recurrence early instead of waiting for a frustrated repeat complaint.$$),
('a2-f-1124','aplus2','core',4,'4.6',$$A customer reports only that 'the internet is broken.' What communication habit helps avoid wasting time chasing the wrong fix?$$,$$Ask clarifying questions before assuming a cause - is it every website or just one, every device or just theirs, did anything change recently - rather than jumping straight to a guessed diagnosis based on a vague description, which risks troubleshooting an entirely different problem than the one actually occurring.$$),
('a2-f-1125','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.6',$$ETA$$,$$Estimated Time of Arrival - the projected time a technician will arrive onsite or a fix/part will be completed; giving an honest ETA, and updating it the moment it changes, is a core expectation-setting habit in support communication.$$),
('a2-f-1126','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.6',$$SLA$$,$$Service Level Agreement - a commitment defining expected response and resolution timeframes for support requests; professional communication means managing customer expectations against the SLA rather than promising faster than what has actually been committed.$$),
('a2-f-1127','aplus2','feynman',4,'4.6',$$Explain why avoiding technical jargon with a non-technical customer is about more than just politeness.$$,$$Model answer: jargon creates a comprehension gap - if the customer doesn't understand what's wrong, they can't meaningfully consent to a repair plan, follow instructions like restarting a service or checking a cable, or accurately describe symptoms if the problem recurs. It also erodes trust, since people who feel talked over rather than informed become less cooperative and more anxious about being taken advantage of. Translating findings into plain language isn't dumbing it down - it's transferring the same information in a form the other person can actually use.$$),
('a2-f-1128','aplus2','feynman',4,'4.6',$$Explain why staying calm with a difficult, angry customer produces a better outcome than being technically 'right' but curt.$$,$$Model answer: an angry customer is usually reacting to frustration and a feeling of being unheard, not just the technical problem itself. Matching their tone or being curt confirms their fear that they aren't being taken seriously, which escalates the conflict and makes them less likely to cooperate with troubleshooting or accept an explanation, even a correct one. Staying calm, listening fully, and acknowledging the frustration defuses the emotional layer first - and that is what actually opens the door to resolving the technical layer efficiently while leaving the customer with a positive impression regardless of how the facts land.$$),
('a2-f-1129','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What file extension identifies a Windows batch script, and what does the line '@echo off' do at its start?$$,$$Batch scripts use the .bat extension. '@echo off' suppresses the automatic echoing of each command to the screen as it executes, so only the script's actual output is displayed rather than every line being run - the leading @ also silences that echo-off command itself.$$),
('a2-f-1130','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What file extension identifies a PowerShell script, and what naming pattern do its built-in commands (cmdlets) follow?$$,$$.ps1 is the PowerShell script extension. Cmdlets follow a Verb-Noun naming pattern, such as Get-Process, Stop-Service, or Set-ExecutionPolicy, which makes their purpose predictable and searchable compared with older, terser command names.$$),
('a2-f-1131','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What file extension identifies a Bash script, and what does the first line '#!/bin/bash' do?$$,$$Bash scripts commonly use the .sh extension. The '#!/bin/bash' shebang line tells the operating system which interpreter should execute the file - here, the Bash shell - so the script can be run directly instead of requiring the user to manually invoke bash on it.$$),
('a2-f-1132','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$Why is Python popular for writing IT automation scripts meant to run on more than one operating system?$$,$$Python is interpreted, with no separate compile step, and its core syntax and standard library behave essentially the same on Windows, Linux, and macOS - so one .py script can often automate a task across all three without rewriting it, unlike batch (Windows-only) or Bash (Linux/macOS-only).$$),
('a2-f-1133','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What was VBScript typically used for, and why has its use declined in modern environments?$$,$$VBScript (.vbs) was widely used for legacy Windows automation, including login scripts and simple administrative tasks. Its use has declined because it's deprecated or blocked in modern browsers and increasingly restricted within Windows itself, largely due to a long history of abuse as a malware-delivery format - PowerShell is now favored for the same automation needs.$$),
('a2-f-1134','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What is a variable in a script, and why is using one better than hard-coding a value?$$,$$A variable is a named container that holds a value - text, a number, a list - which the script can read or change while it runs. Using a variable, such as a target folder path or server name, instead of hard-coding it means the same script can be reused for different inputs by changing the variable once, rather than editing the script's logic everywhere that value appears.$$),
('a2-f-1135','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What does a loop construct (for/while) do in a script, and what is a simple IT use case?$$,$$A loop repeats a block of commands, either a set number of times or while a condition holds. Example: looping through a list of fifty computer names to ping each one and record whether it responded, instead of writing fifty nearly-identical individual commands.$$),
('a2-f-1136','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What does an if/then/else conditional construct do in a script?$$,$$It lets the script branch its behavior based on a test: if a condition is true, run one block of commands; otherwise, run a different block. For example: if a ping succeeds, log the host as online; else, log it as unreachable.$$),
('a2-f-1137','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What is an environment variable, and how does a script benefit from referencing one instead of a fixed value?$$,$$An environment variable is a named value stored by the operating system, such as PATH, USERNAME, or TEMP, that any running process, including a script, can read. Referencing it instead of hard-coding a system-specific detail lets the same script adapt automatically to whatever machine or account it happens to run under.$$),
('a2-f-1138','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What is the purpose of comments in a script, and do they change how it runs?$$,$$Comments - lines starting with # in Bash or Python, or REM in batch - are notes for humans explaining what the code does or why. They are ignored entirely by the interpreter and have no effect on execution; they exist purely so anyone who later reads, maintains, or troubleshoots the script can understand it.$$),
('a2-f-1139','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$Give a realistic IT scenario where a short script saves significant time compared with doing the task by hand.$$,$$Bulk-renaming hundreds of files to a consistent naming convention, or provisioning fifty new user accounts with the same baseline group memberships and home folder - tasks that are repetitive, rule-based, and error-prone by hand are exactly what scripting automates reliably and quickly.$$),
('a2-f-1140','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$Why is running an unsigned script downloaded from an unfamiliar website a security risk, even if it claims to do something helpful?$$,$$An unsigned script has no verified author, so there's no way to confirm it hasn't been tampered with or that it does only what it claims - it could just as easily delete files, exfiltrate data, install malware, or create a backdoor account. Signed scripts, code review, or sourcing from a trusted internal repository all reduce this risk; blindly running an unknown script, especially with elevated privileges, does not.$$),
('a2-f-1141','aplus2','core',4,'4.7',$$What are two scripting best practices that reduce the risk of an automation script causing damage in production?$$,$$Test the script against sample data in an isolated, non-production environment before running it against real systems, and avoid hard-coding credentials or sensitive values directly in the script - use a secure secrets store, or a prompt, instead. Both limit the damage if the script has a bug or is ever exposed or leaked.$$),
('a2-f-1142','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.7',$$CLI$$,$$Command-Line Interface - a text-based interface, such as Bash, PowerShell, or Command Prompt, where scripts and commands are typed and executed, as opposed to a graphical point-and-click interface.$$),
('a2-f-1143','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.7',$$IDE$$,$$Integrated Development Environment - an application, such as VS Code or PyCharm, that combines a code editor, debugger, and other tools in one place, commonly used to write and test scripts before deploying them.$$),
('a2-f-1144','aplus2','acronym',4,'4.7',$$REPL$$,$$Read-Eval-Print Loop - an interactive shell, common in Python and PowerShell, that reads one line of code, evaluates it immediately, prints the result, and loops - useful for testing small pieces of script logic without writing a full file.$$),
('a2-f-1145','aplus2','feynman',4,'4.7',$$Explain why running an unsigned script from an unknown source is risky, even if the script's visible code looks harmless.$$,$$Model answer: what you can read isn't necessarily everything the script does or everything it will do the next time it's fetched. A script can download and execute additional code at runtime, hide a payload through obfuscation, or simply be a slightly modified version of what you actually reviewed if it's hosted somewhere you don't control. An unsigned script also has no verifiable author, so there's no accountability if something goes wrong. The real risk isn't just 'bad code' - it's unverifiable trust: you're choosing to execute, often with your own privileges, instructions from someone you can't confirm and can't hold accountable, which is exactly the access malware needs.$$),
('a2-f-1146','aplus2','feynman',4,'4.7',$$Explain why hard-coding a plaintext administrator password inside an automation script is a bad practice, and what should be done instead.$$,$$Model answer: the script file itself becomes a credential-disclosure risk - anyone who can read it, whether a coworker with repository access, a backup copy, a misconfigured file share, or an attacker who compromises any system storing it, now has that administrator password, and it doesn't rotate or get revoked just because the script is 'internal.' It also means every copy of the script anywhere becomes something that must be tracked and secured. Instead, the script should retrieve the credential at runtime from a secrets manager or a protected credential store, so the password lives in exactly one managed, auditable, rotatable place rather than scattered across every copy of a text file.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('a2-q-1100','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',1,$q$A developer modifies an open-source tool released under the GPL and uses the modified version only inside their own company, with no plan to give it to anyone outside the organization. Under the GPL, are they required to publish their source-code changes?$q$,$q$["Yes, the GPL requires publishing changes the moment the code is modified","No, the obligation to release source code is triggered by distributing the software outside the organization, not by internal modification and use","Yes, but only if the modification exceeds a certain number of lines of code","No, GPL-licensed software can never legally be modified at all"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The GPL's copyleft obligation attaches to distributing the software or a derivative work. Purely internal use and modification, without giving a copy to anyone outside the organization, does not trigger the requirement to release source code.$q$),
('a2-q-1101','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$A vendor's EULA states that the software 'may not be reverse engineered, decompiled, or redistributed.' Which type of license is this EULA most likely describing?$q$,$q$["A commercial/proprietary license","A permissive open-source license","A copyleft open-source license","A public domain dedication"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Prohibiting reverse engineering, decompiling, and redistribution is characteristic of a commercial/proprietary EULA, which restricts what a user may do with the software. Open-source licenses, permissive or copyleft, explicitly permit inspecting and modifying source code, and public domain content carries no such restriction at all.$q$),
('a2-q-1102','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',1,$q$A company takes an MIT-licensed library, modifies it, and ships the modified version inside its own closed-source commercial product without releasing the changes. Is this generally allowed under the MIT license?$q$,$q$["Yes - MIT is a permissive license that allows relicensing a derivative work as proprietary, as long as the original copyright notice is retained","No - MIT is a copyleft license and any derivative must also be released under the MIT license","No - the MIT license forbids any commercial use of the code whatsoever","Yes, but only if the company pays a royalty to the original author"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$MIT is a permissive license: it allows reuse, modification, and even relicensing as proprietary or closed-source software, with the main requirement being to preserve the original copyright and license notice. That is the key contrast with copyleft licenses like the GPL, which would require the derivative to remain open.$q$),
('a2-q-1103','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',3,$q$A technician replaces a failed motherboard in a PC that originally shipped with an OEM copy of Windows. What is the most likely licensing consequence?$q$,$q$["The OEM license may fail to reactivate, since it is typically tied to the original hardware and generally is not transferable to different hardware","Nothing - OEM licenses always transfer freely to any replacement hardware","The license automatically upgrades itself to a retail license","Windows will refuse to boot entirely, regardless of activation status"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$OEM licenses are bound to the first major hardware component they are activated on, commonly the motherboard, and generally cannot be moved to different hardware, unlike a retail or full-use license. A hardware swap like this can force reactivation or require purchasing a new license.$q$),
('a2-q-1104','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',1,$q$A user purchases an e-book that is protected with DRM. Why can't they simply copy the file to a friend's e-reader from a different vendor?$q$,$q$["DRM encrypts or otherwise restricts the content so it only opens through the authorized vendor's app or account, preventing free copying between devices or services","E-books are never sold with any usage restrictions at all","The file format is physically incompatible with every other device on the market","DRM only applies to streaming video and never to e-books"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DRM wraps purchased digital content in restrictions, often encryption tied to an account or authorized app, so it cannot simply be copied and used anywhere. Moving it usually requires going through the vendor's own transfer or authorization process, if that is even permitted.$q$),
('a2-q-1105','aplus2',4,'4.5','multi',2,$q$An organization is evaluating whether to move from individual retail licenses to a volume license for its office suite. Select the TWO benefits this typically provides. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Centralized activation and tracking of how many seats are deployed against the agreement","Automatic release of the software's source code to the organization","The ability to deploy the same agreement's terms across many machines under one purchase","Complete exemption from any future licensing compliance audits"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,2]'::jsonb,$q$Volume licensing centralizes activation and tracking, and it lets one agreement cover deployment across many machines or seats. It has nothing to do with source-code disclosure, which is an open-source licensing concept, and it does not exempt an organization from being audited for compliance.$q$),
('a2-q-1106','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',1,$q$An employee asks the help desk to install a company-licensed application on their personally-owned laptop so they can work from home. What should the technician check FIRST?$q$,$q$["Whether the software's license terms and the organization's personally-owned device policy actually permit installation on a non-company-owned device","Whether the employee's laptop has enough free disk space for the install","Whether the employee prefers a light or dark interface theme","Whether the laptop's manufacturer matches the company's standard hardware vendor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Many software licenses are scoped to company-owned assets, and personally-owned device policies often add requirements, such as accepting a management profile or remote-wipe terms, before IT may install or manage anything on the device. That policy and license check comes before any technical step.$q$),
('a2-q-1107','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',3,$q$While repairing a customer's laptop, a technician notices files that appear to be illegal content. What is the correct FIRST action?$q$,$q$["Stop working on the device, avoid opening or further accessing the files, and report it through the proper channel while preserving the device as found","Quietly delete the files so the customer never finds out, then continue the repair","Take screenshots of the files as personal proof, then continue repairing normally","Confront the customer immediately and demand an explanation before doing anything else"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The moment prohibited content is suspected, a technician must stop, avoid further access to protect any evidence and avoid any appearance of viewing or distributing it, preserve the device intact, and escalate through the defined reporting channel. Deleting the files, copying them, or confronting the customer all destroy evidence or overstep the technician's authority.$q$),
('a2-q-1108','aplus2',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$A device is secured as potential evidence after prohibited content is discovered on it. Two weeks later, a question arises about whether anyone accessed it after it was secured. What documentation would resolve this?$q$,$q$["Chain-of-custody records showing every person who possessed or accessed the device, and when","The original sales receipt for the device","The device's warranty registration card","A backup of the device's photo library"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Chain of custody is precisely the unbroken record of who had possession of a piece of evidence and when, including any access - it is what proves, or disproves, whether an item was handled properly after being secured.$q$),
('a2-q-1109','aplus2',4,'4.5','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO details that must appear in incident response documentation for a discovered policy violation. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["The date and time the issue was first noticed, and by whom","The technician's personal opinion about the customer's character","Every action taken during the response, in the order it happened","The brand of coffee the technician was drinking during the shift"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,2]'::jsonb,$q$Solid incident documentation records objective facts: when the issue surfaced and who reported it, plus a clear, ordered account of every response action taken, which supports any later legal, HR, or audit review. Personal opinions and irrelevant details have no place in the record and can undermine its credibility.$q$),
('a2-q-1110','aplus2',4,'4.5','matching',2,$q$Match each licensing or policy term to its best description.$q$,$q${"left":["Copyleft open-source license","Permissive open-source license","Commercial/proprietary EULA","Digital Rights Management (DRM)"],"right":["Requires a distributed derivative work to remain open under the same license","Allows relicensing a modified version as closed-source with minimal conditions","Restricts reverse engineering, redistribution, and the number of permitted installations","Uses encryption or activation checks to restrict copying or moving purchased digital content"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$Copyleft licenses such as the GPL force distributed derivatives to stay open; permissive licenses such as MIT or BSD allow closed-source reuse; a commercial EULA restricts reverse engineering, redistribution, and install counts; and DRM technically restricts copying or transferring purchased content.$q$),
('a2-q-1111','aplus2',4,'4.5','ordering',3,$q$A technician discovers what appears to be illegal content on a customer's device during a repair. Place the following actions in the correct order.$q$,$q$["Document who discovered the content, and the date and time of discovery","Stop working on the device and do not open or view the suspicious files any further","Report the discovery through the proper channel (supervisor, legal, or law enforcement) and hand off the device","Secure and label the device so it is not touched by anyone else, preserving it exactly as found"]$q$::jsonb,'[1,3,0,2]'::jsonb,$q$The response starts by ceasing any further contact with the material, then physically securing and labeling the device to protect the evidence, then documenting who discovered it and when, and finally escalating through the correct channel so someone with the authority to act takes over. Reporting before securing, or securing before stopping access, risks contaminating the evidence.$q$),
('a2-q-1112','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',1,$q$A customer is explaining a problem in detail over the phone. What is the BEST practice for the technician while the customer is talking?$q$,$q$["Listen fully without interrupting, then restate the problem to confirm understanding","Interrupt as soon as the likely cause seems obvious, to save time","Start typing ticket notes and only listen partially","Redirect the conversation to a different, unrelated topic to build rapport"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Active listening - letting the customer finish, then restating the problem in your own words - confirms you understood the actual issue before acting on an assumption, and it makes the customer feel heard, which builds cooperation for the rest of the interaction.$q$),
('a2-q-1113','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',1,$q$A technician tells a non-technical customer, 'Your DNS resolver cache had a stale record, so I flushed it.' The customer looks confused. What should the technician have done instead?$q$,$q$["Explain it in plain language, such as saying the computer had outdated saved information about that website's address and it was cleared out so it can look it up fresh","Repeat the exact same technical explanation, only more slowly","Assume the customer doesn't need to understand and move on to the next task","Write the technical explanation down so the customer can research it later"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Translating a technical concept into plain, relatable language, without simply repeating jargon more slowly or leaving the customer confused, is the core of avoiding-jargon communication - it keeps the customer informed and comfortable rather than making them feel talked down to.$q$),
('a2-q-1114','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',1,$q$A technician is running twenty minutes behind schedule for a customer's onsite appointment. What is the professional action?$q$,$q$["Call or message the customer proactively with an updated arrival time and a brief reason","Say nothing and simply arrive late without comment","Cancel the appointment entirely to avoid the awkward conversation","Wait until the customer calls to complain, then explain the delay"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Proactively communicating a delay respects the customer's time and lets them plan around it, rather than leaving them wondering or frustrated. Silence, outright cancellation, or waiting to be confronted are all worse for the relationship than an honest, timely heads-up.$q$),
('a2-q-1115','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',3,$q$After diagnosing a hardware failure that requires a backordered part, what is the BEST way for a technician to set expectations with the customer about the timeline?$q$,$q$["Give a specific, realistic estimate based on the part's actual lead time, noting that it may be updated if anything changes","Promise it will be fixed 'very soon' to keep the customer happy in the moment","Refuse to give any timeframe at all, since nothing is fully certain","Tell the customer the part will arrive tomorrow even though the vendor quoted a week"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Setting expectations means giving honest, specific information, even an estimate with a caveat that it could shift, rather than a vague reassurance or, worse, a promise the technician already knows is unrealistic. Trust is built by expectations that hold up, not by whatever sounds best in the moment.$q$),
('a2-q-1116','aplus2',4,'4.6','multi',2,$q$A customer becomes visibly angry and starts raising their voice at the technician. Select the TWO appropriate responses. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Stay calm and let the customer finish expressing their frustration before responding","Acknowledge their frustration and redirect the conversation toward a concrete next step","Raise your own voice to match their energy and assert control of the conversation","Tell the customer they need to calm down before you will help them"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$De-escalation works by staying calm, letting the person be heard, acknowledging the emotion as valid, and then steering toward action - not by matching their volume or issuing a demand that they change their behavior first, both of which tend to escalate rather than defuse the situation.$q$),
('a2-q-1117','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',3,$q$A customer insists a slowdown is caused by 'the new update you installed,' but the technician's logs clearly show the slowdown began two days before that update was applied. What is the BEST way to handle this disagreement?$q$,$q$["Calmly and respectfully walk the customer through the timeline evidence, and offer to keep investigating other causes together","Tell the customer flatly that they are wrong and end the conversation","Agree the update caused it, even though the evidence says otherwise, just to avoid conflict","Ignore the customer's theory entirely and refuse to discuss it further"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Professional disagreement means presenting the evidence respectfully and keeping the customer engaged in solving the actual problem, rather than being blunt and dismissive, which damages the relationship, or falsely agreeing, which abandons the real fix and misleads the customer.$q$),
('a2-q-1118','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',1,$q$While working at a customer's desk, a technician's personal phone buzzes repeatedly with texts. What is the professional response?$q$,$q$["Silence the phone and keep full attention on the customer and the task at hand","Answer the texts quickly between troubleshooting steps since it only takes a second","Put the customer's work on hold and step outside to reply","Show the customer the texts to explain why there's an interruption"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Avoiding distractions, such as silencing personal devices and staying focused, signals respect for the customer's time and the seriousness of the work, and it prevents avoidable mistakes caused by divided attention.$q$),
('a2-q-1119','aplus2',4,'4.6','multi',2,$q$A technician notices confidential financial figures displayed on a customer's monitor while repairing their PC. Select the TWO appropriate ways to handle this. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Treat the information as confidential and do not discuss it with coworkers or on social media","Share it only through official channels, and only if it is genuinely relevant to resolving or escalating the issue","Mention it casually to coworkers later since it seemed interesting","Bring it up directly with the customer to make conversation"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Confidentiality means the information stays out of casual conversation and social media, and is only shared through official channels when it is actually relevant to the task at hand - not brought up socially with the customer or mentioned informally to coworkers.$q$),
('a2-q-1120','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$A support ticket is marked resolved after a fix is applied remotely. What professional step is often overlooked but improves customer satisfaction?$q$,$q$["A brief proactive follow-up to confirm the fix is holding and the customer is satisfied","Closing the ticket immediately with no further contact at all","Sending the customer an extremely long satisfaction survey","Waiting for the customer to reopen the ticket if something goes wrong"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A short proactive follow-up closes the loop, demonstrates ongoing care, and catches a recurring issue early, rather than leaving the customer to notice a failure and re-escalate on their own, which reads as indifference.$q$),
('a2-q-1121','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$A customer vaguely reports that 'the network is down.' Rather than immediately assuming a specific cause, what should the technician do first?$q$,$q$["Ask clarifying questions, such as which devices or sites are affected, when it started, and what changed recently, before diagnosing","Immediately restart the main office router without asking anything","Assume it is the same issue as last week and apply the same fix again","Tell the customer to try again later without any investigation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Avoiding assumptions and asking clarifying questions ensures the technician diagnoses the actual scope and cause of the problem, rather than wasting time, and the customer's patience, chasing an incorrect guess based on a vague description.$q$),
('a2-q-1122','aplus2',4,'4.6','mcq',1,$q$Which of the following BEST demonstrates professionalism regarding cultural sensitivity in customer support?$q$,$q$["Using neutral, respectful, work-focused language regardless of the customer's background","Making light jokes about a customer's accent to build rapport","Assuming every customer shares the technician's own cultural references","Adjusting technical accuracy based on assumptions about the customer's background"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Sticking to neutral, respectful, work-focused language treats every customer with the same professionalism regardless of background, avoiding the risk of unintentionally offensive jokes or assumptions that damage trust and reputation.$q$),
('a2-q-1123','aplus2',4,'4.6','matching',2,$q$Match each communication technique to its purpose.$q$,$q${"left":["Active listening","Avoiding jargon","Setting expectations","De-escalation"],"right":["Confirms the technician understood the customer's actual problem","Ensures a non-technical customer can follow the explanation","Gives the customer an honest, realistic timeline for the fix","Calms an angry customer so troubleshooting can proceed productively"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$Active listening confirms accurate understanding of the issue; avoiding jargon keeps explanations usable by a non-technical audience; setting expectations gives honest timelines; and de-escalation defuses emotional conflict so the actual technical work can move forward.$q$),
('a2-q-1124','aplus2',4,'4.6','ordering',3,$q$Place the following steps for handling a difficult, angry customer in the correct order.$q$,$q$["Acknowledge and validate the frustration in your own words","Propose a concrete next step or timeline and follow through on it","Let the customer fully express their frustration without interrupting","Restate the underlying technical problem to confirm shared understanding"]$q$::jsonb,'[2,0,3,1]'::jsonb,$q$De-escalation starts by letting the emotion be expressed and heard, then explicitly acknowledging it so the customer feels understood, then confirming the actual technical problem so nothing is chased on a wrong assumption, and finally moving to a concrete, followed-through action - skipping the emotional steps and jumping straight to a fix often reignites the frustration.$q$),
('a2-q-1125','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',1,$q$Which file extension identifies a native Windows batch script?$q$,$q$[".bat",".sh",".py",".vbs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Batch files use the .bat extension and are interpreted by cmd.exe. .sh identifies a Bash script for Linux/macOS, .py identifies Python, and .vbs identifies VBScript, a different Windows scripting language than batch.$q$),
('a2-q-1126','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',1,$q$A PowerShell script file is shared with a technician for review. Which file extension should it have?$q$,$q$[".ps1",".bat",".sh",".py"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$.ps1 is the standard PowerShell script extension. .bat identifies batch, .sh identifies Bash, and .py identifies Python - each scripting language has its own distinct extension.$q$),
('a2-q-1127','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',2,$q$A technician writes a script intended to run under Bash on a Linux server. Which line should appear as the very first line of the file to correctly identify the interpreter?$q$,$q$["#!/bin/bash","@echo off","<?php","REM Bash script"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The shebang line #!/bin/bash tells the operating system which interpreter should run the script. '@echo off' is a batch-specific directive, '<?php' opens a PHP code block, and 'REM' begins a batch comment - none of these are valid ways to declare a Bash interpreter.$q$),
('a2-q-1128','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',3,$q$A team needs one automation script that will run unmodified on their Windows, Linux, and macOS technicians' machines. Which scripting language is the BEST fit given this requirement?$q$,$q$["Python","Windows batch","VBScript","A mix of batch and VBScript"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Python is interpreted, and its core syntax and standard library behave consistently across Windows, Linux, and macOS, making a single script portable. Batch and VBScript are Windows-only technologies and will not run natively on Linux or macOS at all.$q$),
('a2-q-1129','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',2,$q$A technician needs to check whether each of two hundred hostnames responds to a ping and log the result for every one. Which basic scripting construct is BEST suited to repeating the same check across the whole list?$q$,$q$["A loop (for/while)","A single if/then statement","A block of comments","A hard-coded value"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A loop repeats a block of commands across a list or while a condition holds, which is exactly what's needed to run the same ping-and-log check against two hundred different hostnames without writing two hundred near-identical lines by hand.$q$),
('a2-q-1130','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',1,$q$What is the purpose of an if/then/else construct in a script?$q$,$q$["To make the script take a different action depending on whether a condition is true or false","To repeat a block of commands a fixed number of times","To store a text or numeric value for later use","To document what the script does for future readers"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An if/then/else is a conditional branch: it tests something and runs one block of code if the condition holds, and a different block otherwise. Repetition is a loop's job, storage is a variable's job, and documentation is a comment's job.$q$),
('a2-q-1131','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',1,$q$A script needs to reference the currently logged-in username without hard-coding it, so the same script works correctly no matter which technician runs it. What should the script reference instead?$q$,$q$["An environment variable, such as %USERNAME% or $USER","A hard-coded administrator account name","A comment describing the intended user","A randomly generated string"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Environment variables such as %USERNAME% on Windows or $USER on Linux/macOS are supplied by the operating system for whichever account is actually running the process, so referencing them lets the same script adapt automatically instead of being hard-coded to one specific user.$q$),
('a2-q-1132','aplus2',4,'4.7','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO practices that reduce the risk of an automation script causing damage when it's deployed. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Test the script against sample data in a non-production environment first","Store required credentials in a secrets manager rather than hard-coding them in the script","Disable all logging so the script runs faster","Grant the script's service account full domain administrator rights by default, just in case"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Testing in an isolated environment catches bugs before they touch production, and keeping credentials out of the script file, using a managed secrets store instead, prevents a leaked or shared script from exposing a password. Disabling logging removes the ability to diagnose failures, and granting excessive privileges 'just in case' massively increases the damage a bug or compromise can cause - both increase risk rather than reduce it.$q$),
('a2-q-1133','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',3,$q$PowerShell's execution policy is set to 'Restricted' on a workstation by default. What is the security purpose of this setting?$q$,$q$["It prevents PowerShell scripts from running at all unless the policy is changed, reducing the chance a malicious or accidental script executes silently","It restricts PowerShell to read-only commands with no ability to modify files","It permanently allows only scripts signed by Microsoft to ever run","It disables PowerShell's command history for privacy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The execution policy governs whether, and under what conditions, .ps1 scripts are allowed to run - 'Restricted' blocks script execution entirely by default, a deliberate speed bump against a malicious or accidental script running unnoticed. It does not limit interactive commands to read-only, and 'Restricted' is not the same as an AllSigned-only policy.$q$),
('a2-q-1134','aplus2',4,'4.7','mcq',1,$q$What is the purpose of comments in a script, such as lines starting with # in Python or Bash?$q$,$q$["To explain what the code does for anyone reading or maintaining it later, without affecting how the script runs","To make the script execute faster","To mark which lines should run first","To automatically generate separate documentation files"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Comments are notes for human readers - the interpreter ignores them entirely, so they have zero effect on execution speed or order. Their value is purely in making the script's logic understandable to whoever maintains it later, including the original author.$q$),
('a2-q-1135','aplus2',4,'4.7','multi',2,$q$A newly written provisioning script creates fifty user accounts but crashes partway through after creating only thirty, with no indication of which ones succeeded. Select the TWO things that should have been built into the script to prevent this problem. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Logging that records the outcome of each account creation as it happens","Error handling that reports where the script stopped if it fails","Removing all comments to simplify the code","Running the script with a longer, randomly generated variable name"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Without per-step logging and error handling, a mid-run failure leaves no record of what succeeded, forcing a manual audit of all fifty accounts. Recording each outcome and handling errors gracefully would show exactly where to resume - removing comments and renaming variables have no bearing on fault tolerance.$q$),
('a2-q-1136','aplus2',4,'4.7','matching',2,$q$Match each script file extension to the scripting language or runtime it is associated with.$q$,$q${"left":[".bat",".ps1",".sh",".py",".vbs"],"right":["Windows batch, run by cmd.exe","PowerShell","Bash","Python","VBScript"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3,4]'::jsonb,$q$.bat is a Windows batch file run by cmd.exe, .ps1 is a PowerShell script, .sh is a Bash (or general POSIX shell) script, .py is Python, and .vbs is VBScript - each extension maps to exactly one of these scripting languages.$q$),
('a2-q-1137','aplus2',4,'4.7','ordering',3,$q$Place the following steps for safely introducing a new automation script into production in the correct order.$q$,$q$["Get change-management approval to run it against production systems","Write the script and document what it does and why","Run it against a small pilot group while monitoring for errors","Test it against sample data in an isolated, non-production environment","Deploy it broadly with logging enabled and update the documentation and knowledge base"]$q$::jsonb,'[1,3,0,2,4]'::jsonb,$q$A safe rollout starts with writing and documenting the script, then proving it works against sample data somewhere mistakes are cheap, then getting the change approved through the proper process, then limiting the blast radius with a small monitored pilot before going broad, and finally deploying widely with logging and updated documentation so the next person understands what is running and why. Jumping straight from 'written' to 'deployed broadly' is exactly how an untested script causes a production-wide incident.$q$);
